"# Designed Around People\n*Exploring human connection, identity, and agency through Value Sensitive Design.*\n\n**INFO 464 | Value Sensitive Design Portfolio**\n\nTechnology is often designed to make our lives easier, whether that's recommending music or helping us share our work with others. However, convenience isn't the only thing that matters when designing an experience.\n\nThrough two individual projects, I wanted to explore how technology influences the ways we connect with others, express ourselves, and make choices. My Spotify AI DJ zine examines what can get lost when music discovery becomes increasingly automated, while my personal portfolio website explores how I can represent myself online while maintaining control over what I share.\n\nAlthough these projects are different, both made me think more critically about whose values are prioritized in design and whose experiences might be overlooked.\n\n---\n\n# 01. Beyond the Algorithm\n## The Human Side of Music Discovery\n\n**Project:** Spotify AI DJ Zine  \n**Course:** INFO 464  \n**Role:** Individual creator  \n**Focus:** Human connection, autonomy, personalization, creativity\n\n### The Problem\n\nSpotify's AI DJ uses listening habits to recommend music and create a personalized listening experience. While this makes listening to music more convenient, I've noticed that its recommendations don't always feel personal to me, even when they're based on songs I already enjoy.\n\nThis made me think about how discovering music through an algorithm differs from discovering it through another person. A friend might recommend a song because it reminds them of a shared memory, while a DJ can respond to the energy of a crowd. Those experiences involve context and human connection that an algorithm might not fully capture.\n\nMy zine explores the tension between automated personalization and the social experiences that make music meaningful.\n\n### My Design\n\nFor my zine, I placed Spotify's AI DJ symbol in the center of a collage surrounded by artist posters and album imagery. I wanted to draw attention to the people and creativity behind the music being recommended, rather than focusing entirely on the AI system delivering it.\n\nI also added playback controls to make the collage resemble a music player and connect the artwork to the experience of listening to music.\n\nThe goal wasn't to redesign Spotify's interface, but to question which values are emphasized when music discovery becomes increasingly automated.\n\n### Stakeholders and Values\n\n**Listeners \u2014 Autonomy, discovery, and connection**\n\nListeners benefit from convenient recommendations, but they may also value exploring unfamiliar music, making their own choices, and connecting with others through shared music.\n\n**Artists \u2014 Creativity, recognition, and visibility**\n\nArtists create the music that recommendation systems distribute. How songs are selected and presented can influence which artists receive attention and how listeners engage with their work.\n\n**Friends and music communities \u2014 Connection and shared experiences**\n\nSharing music can be a way of exchanging personal stories, building relationships, and introducing others to different cultures and experiences.\n\n**Spotify \u2014 Personalization, convenience, and engagement**\n\nSpotify benefits from creating an experience that feels relevant and easy to use, encouraging listeners to continue using the platform.\n\nThese values aren't necessarily incompatible, but prioritizing convenience and personalization doesn't automatically preserve the social aspects of discovering music.\n\n### Tripartite Methodology\n\nValue Sensitive Design uses three complementary approaches: conceptual, empirical, and technical investigations. These help designers identify relevant values, understand how stakeholders experience them, and examine how technology supports or challenges those values.\n\n**Conceptual Investigation**\n\nI explored the difference between personalization and meaningful discovery. A recommendation can match someone's listening history without creating the same experience as receiving a song from a friend.\n\nThis made me question whether a system can be considered personal simply because it predicts what someone might enjoy. My zine highlights the people and creative experiences behind music as a way of challenging this assumption.\n\n**Empirical Investigation**\n\nMy original zine was based on my personal experiences with Spotify's AI DJ rather than formal user research.\n\nIf I continued this project, I would want to interview listeners about how they discover new music, whether they prefer recommendations from people or algorithms, and what makes a recommendation feel meaningful.\n\nThis would help me understand whether other listeners share my perspective or value different aspects of AI-powered music discovery.\n\n**Technical Investigation**\n\nI examined how AI DJ presents automated recommendations through a music-listening interface. While listeners have familiar playback controls, those controls don't necessarily explain why particular songs were recommended.\n\nMy zine uses playback controls as a visual reference rather than proposing a functional redesign.\n\nA future design could explore giving listeners more control over recommendations, providing explanations for song selections, or incorporating recommendations shared by friends.\n\n### Value Sensitive Design Methods\n\n#### Method 1: Stakeholder Analysis\n\nI identified the people and groups directly or indirectly affected by AI-powered music recommendations.\n\nListeners are direct stakeholders because they interact with AI DJ, while artists and music communities can be indirectly affected by how music is distributed and discovered.\n\nThis analysis helped me recognize that music recommendation systems influence more than individual preferences. They also affect artistic visibility and the social experiences surrounding music.\n\n**Visual:** Stakeholder map connecting listeners, artists, music communities, and Spotify with their values.\n\n#### Method 2: Value Scenarios\n\nI created two hypothetical scenarios to explore how different recommendation experiences might support different values.\n\n**Scenario A: Discovering music through AI**\n\nA student opens Spotify's AI DJ while studying. The system recommends songs similar to their listening history, making it easy to enjoy music without searching for anything. Although the songs match their preferences, the student doesn't necessarily learn anything about the artists or why those songs might be meaningful to others.\n\n**Scenario B: Discovering music through a friend**\n\nA friend shares a song and explains that it reminds them of a particular memory. Even though the song is outside the student's usual preferences, the story makes them curious enough to listen.\n\nComparing these scenarios helped me recognize that discovering music isn't always about finding the most accurate recommendation. Sometimes, the story or relationship behind a recommendation makes the experience more meaningful.\n\n**Visual:** Two-panel illustration comparing algorithmic and social music discovery.\n\n#### Method 3: Envisioning Cards\n\nEnvisioning Cards are a VSD method that encourages designers to consider the broader and longer-term effects of technology, including how it might influence stakeholders, values, and everyday experiences.\n\nFor this portfolio, I extended my original zine by considering how AI-powered music discovery could change the way people share and experience music over time.\n\n**Direct and indirect stakeholders**\n\nAI DJ directly affects listeners, but it can also influence artists and music communities. If more people rely on automated recommendations, the ways artists gain visibility and listeners discover unfamiliar music could change.\n\n**Long-term implications**\n\nI considered what music discovery might look like if AI recommendations became the primary way people found new songs. While this could make music more accessible and convenient, it might also reduce opportunities for spontaneous recommendations and conversations between people.\n\n**Pervasiveness**\n\nMusic recommendation systems are already integrated into everyday activities such as studying, commuting, and exercising. As these systems become more common, their influence could extend beyond what people listen to and shape how they develop musical tastes.\n\nThis exploration made me think about how AI could support human connection rather than replace the social experiences associated with discovering music.\n\n**Visual:** A three-part diagram illustrating stakeholders, long-term implications, and pervasiveness.\n\n### Takeaways and Future Work\n\nCreating this zine helped me realize that personalization and human connection aren't necessarily the same thing. AI can make discovering music easier, but it doesn't always recreate the meaning that comes from sharing music with another person.\n\nOne limitation of my work is that it reflects my own perspective as a listener. I haven't conducted research to determine whether other users experience AI DJ in the same way.\n\nIf I continued this project, I would want to explore how AI-powered recommendations could incorporate more opportunities for human connection without losing the convenience that makes them useful.\n\nRather than replacing algorithmic discovery, I'm interested in how recommendations from people and AI could complement each other.\n\n---\n\n# 02. Designing My Digital Identity\n## A Personal Portfolio Through Value Sensitive Design\n\n**Project:** Personal Portfolio Website  \n**Role:** Independent designer and developer  \n**Focus:** Self-expression, accessibility, privacy, autonomy  \n**Artifact:** Personal website\n\n### The Problem\n\nA personal portfolio is supposed to communicate who someone is and what they've worked on, but deciding how to represent yourself online isn't always straightforward.\n\nWhen designing my portfolio, I wanted something that reflected both my interests in software engineering and design. I didn't want it to feel like a generic professional website, but I also needed it to be understandable and easy for visitors to navigate.\n\nThis created an interesting design challenge: how could I express my personality while making sure the website remained useful and accessible to different audiences?\n\n### My Design\n\nI designed my portfolio around a scrapbook-inspired digital desktop, combining soft colors, editorial typography, decorative illustrations, and desktop-style windows.\n\nThe design reflects my interest in both technical and creative work. I wanted visitors to see more than a collection of projects and understand some of my personality through the experience of navigating the website.\n\nAt the same time, I included recognizable navigation, project descriptions, and clear links so visitors could find the information they needed.\n\n### Stakeholders and Values\n\n**Myself \u2014 Identity, autonomy, and privacy**\n\nAs the creator of the website, I want control over how I represent myself, which projects I share, and what information I make public.\n\n**Recruiters and hiring managers \u2014 Clarity, efficiency, and trust**\n\nRecruiters need to understand my experience and contributions without navigating an unnecessarily complicated interface.\n\n**Other visitors \u2014 Usability and accessibility**\n\nClassmates, instructors, and other visitors may have different reasons for exploring my website and different needs when interacting with it.\n\n**People using assistive technologies \u2014 Accessibility and equitable access**\n\nVisitors using keyboards, screen readers, or reduced-motion settings should be able to access the website without depending on decorative or interactive elements.\n\n### Tripartite Methodology\n\n**Conceptual Investigation**\n\nI considered the tension between self-expression and usability. A portfolio should reflect the person who created it, but an overly decorative design could make important information harder to find.\n\nI also thought about privacy and autonomy. Having a personal website doesn't mean every project or part of my identity needs to be publicly visible.\n\n**Empirical Investigation**\n\nMy portfolio was primarily developed through my own design decisions and iterations rather than formal user research.\n\nFuture research could involve asking recruiters, students, or other visitors to navigate the website and identify areas that feel confusing or difficult to use.\n\nThis would help me understand whether the website communicates what I intend it to communicate.\n\n**Technical Investigation**\n\nI developed my website using React, TypeScript, and a Next.js-compatible framework.\n\nThe website includes responsive layouts, keyboard focus indicators, descriptive image alternatives, a skip link, and reduced-motion support. These decisions were intended to make the website more accessible to different visitors.\n\nHowever, implementing accessibility features doesn't automatically mean a website is fully accessible. Additional testing would be needed to evaluate how well these features work in practice.\n\n### Value Sensitive Design Methods\n\n#### Method 1: Stakeholder Analysis\n\nI identified different groups who might visit my website and considered how their needs could conflict.\n\nFor example, I value creative self-expression, while a recruiter might prioritize finding relevant technical experience quickly.\n\nRecognizing these differences helped me think about how to balance personality with clear information architecture.\n\n**Visual:** Stakeholder diagram showing myself, recruiters, instructors, and visitors with accessibility needs.\n\n#### Method 2: Value Scenarios\n\nI considered two hypothetical experiences to explore how different visitors might interact with my portfolio.\n\n**Scenario A: A recruiter visiting my website**\n\nA recruiter has limited time and wants to quickly understand my technical experience. The scrapbook design communicates personality, but the navigation and project descriptions need to remain clear enough to support their goal.\n\n**Scenario B: Navigating without a mouse**\n\nA visitor uses Tab to move between links and Enter to open them, instead of using a mouse. The \u201cSkip to projects\u201d link lets them jump straight to the work. Decorative elements should not get in the way.\n\nThese scenarios helped me recognize that visitors can experience the same website differently depending on their needs and goals.\n\n**Visual:** Two annotated screenshots showing navigation and accessibility considerations.\n\n#### Method 3: Value-Oriented Design Analysis\n\nI revisited several design decisions through the values they support.\n\n- **Scrapbook aesthetic:** Supports self-expression and personal identity.\n- **Clear project navigation:** Supports usability and efficiency.\n- **Keyboard focus indicators and skip links:** Support accessibility.\n- **Theme customization:** Supports user preference and control.\n- **Selective project visibility:** Supports privacy and autonomy.\n\nThis analysis helped me recognize that even small interface decisions can reflect broader values.\n\n**Visual:** Annotated screenshots of the actual portfolio, connecting interface elements with their associated values.\n\n### A Design Decision: Keeping My Class Work Unlisted\n\nOne particularly relevant decision was creating this INFO 464 portfolio as an unlisted page rather than including it in my main project navigation.\n\nI wanted my instructor to be able to review my work without necessarily presenting every class assignment to recruiters or other visitors.\n\nThis reflects a tension between visibility and privacy. A portfolio is designed to make work accessible to others, but I still value having control over which work is highlighted publicly.\n\nAn unlisted page isn't completely private, since anyone with the direct link could access it. However, keeping it outside my main navigation and search indexing gives me more control over how the work is presented.\n\n### Takeaways and Future Work\n\nDesigning my portfolio helped me realize that even a personal website involves balancing values that don't always align perfectly.\n\nI wanted the website to feel creative and personal, but I also had to consider whether visitors could easily understand and navigate it.\n\nOne limitation is that I haven't conducted formal accessibility testing or usability research with different visitor groups.\n\nIn the future, I would want to test the website with people who have different navigation preferences and accessibility needs, then use their feedback to refine the experience.\n\n---\n\n# Final Reflection\n## Designing With Values in Mind\n\nLooking at these projects together helped me recognize how much design influences the ways people interact with technology.\n\nWith Spotify's AI DJ, I questioned whether convenience and personalization always lead to meaningful experiences. With my personal portfolio, I explored how self-expression can be balanced with accessibility, privacy, and the needs of different visitors.\n\nBoth projects made me think more critically about whose values are prioritized in a design and whose experiences might be overlooked.\n\nOne of my biggest takeaways from Value Sensitive Design is that there isn't always one perfect solution that satisfies every stakeholder. Instead, designers need to recognize tensions between values, make intentional decisions, and remain open to revisiting those decisions as they learn more.\n\nFor me, designing around people means thinking beyond whether a technology works and considering what kinds of experiences, relationships, and choices it supports.\n\n---\n\n### Attribution\n\nBoth case studies present individual work. The Spotify AI DJ zine is my original collage and critical interpretation of an existing feature, incorporating third-party artist imagery and Spotify branding. My personal portfolio was designed and developed by me with AI-assisted coding support. Neither Spotify nor the featured artists are affiliated with this portfolio."